+++ b/docs/restricted-tracks.md
@@ -0,0 +1,42 @@
+# Restricted Tracks
+
+A track carries a visibility on its on-chain leaf. Storage nodes serve a restricted track's bytes only to a reader presenting a valid owner-signed grant. Access follows control of the tape, not membership in any service. Restricted says nothing about confidentiality from nodes: that is client-side encryption, a separate content format, which rides on ordinary tracks of either visibility.
+
+| piece | where | shape |
+|---|---|---|
+| leaf field | `lib/core/src/track/types.rs` | `visibility: u64` beside `kind` and `state`, committed by `get_hash()` under `TRACK_LEAF_V1`, leaf 136 to 144 bytes |
+| enum | same | `TrackVisibility { Public = 0, Restricted }`, `repr(u64)`, anything not readable as Public counts as restricted |
+| wire | `solana/api/src/instruction/track.rs` | one visibility byte in the `TrackWrite` header, widened like `kind` |
+| tape account | `solana/api/src/state/tape.rs` | `grant_generation: u64`, starts at 0 |
+| revoke | `solana/programs/tapedrive/src/tape/grant.rs` | `BumpGrantGeneration`, authorized by `authorize_tape_operator`, about 3.5k cu |
+| grant | `lib/core/src/grant.rs` | fixed layout under `GRANT_V1`, ed25519 by the tape authority or delegate |
+| carrier | `network/protocol/src/api/grant.rs` | `x-tape-read-grant` header, base64 of the packed struct |
+| reader door | same | `x-tape-read-expiry` unix seconds, `x-tape-read-signature` base64 of 64 bytes |
+| sdk | `sdk/src/write_options.rs`, `sdk/src/read_options.rs` | `WriteOptions.visibility`, `ReadOptions.grant`, `Tapedrive::new_reader` for the mTLS identity |
+| cli | `tools/tape` | `object put --restricted`, `object sync --restricted`, `grant`, `grant --all-tracks`, `revoke-grants` |
+
+## Grant
+
+| field | bytes | meaning |
+|---|---|---|
+| tape | 32 | tape address |
+| key | 32 | track key hash within the tape |
+| reader | 32 | reader's network TLS pubkey |
+| signer | 32 | granting authority or delegate |
+| grant_generation | 8 | tape's generation at issuance |
+| expiry | 8 | unix seconds |
+| signature | 64 | ed25519 over `GRANT_V1 || fields` |
+
+A key of all zeros is the wildcard: that grant opens every track on its tape, which is what a streamed object needs, since each chunk is a track with its own key. A grant scopes to the key hash, so a put-over under the same name keeps it valid. Binding to the reader's TLS pubkey kills replay without nonces. A node checks one hop from state it already holds through replay, no RPC: the signer passes `Tape::is_operator`, the generation matches the tape's, the expiry is ahead of the local clock, the reader equals the request's mTLS pubkey, the key matches the requested track. Revocation is the generation counter: one bump invalidates every grant outstanding on the tape. A bump reaches authorization when its block is ingested, so a revoked reader keeps fetch for the pending window.
+
+## Reader door
+
+A node reads the reader's identity off the client certificate it terminated. Gateway clients, browsers included, have no certificate, so on the gateway the reader signs the call: `x-tape-read-grant` carries the grant, `x-tape-read-expiry` the unix second it stops being good for, and `x-tape-read-signature` an ed25519 signature over `READ_V1 || method || 0x00 || path || 0x00 || expiry`, the path without its query string and the method uppercased. The gateway checks the grant exactly as a node does, against the tape from replay and the local clock, then checks the signature against the key the grant names, and refuses at most 300 seconds of expiry ahead of now, so a captured header set opens one call for one short window. Nodes stay mTLS-only; the gateway is the client-facing door and holds a grant of its own for the fetch, either configured in `gateway.grants` or, for a tape that delegates to it, signed on the spot for its own mTLS identity.
+
+## Enforcement
+
+Every surface that can hand out track bytes gates on `is_restricted()`: node slice, track data, and repair routes (an active peer owning a spool in the track's group is exempt, so sync and repair stay untouched), gateway object, track, slice, and site routes (through the reader door above), and the S3 surface, where a restricted read needs a credential with the read capability whose policy admits the bucket. Authorization runs ahead of the slice cache because the cache key is identity-blind. Metadata stays public by design: existence, key hash, size, commitment, listings. Replay carries visibility through capture, the pending index, and pending tape info, so the checks hold inside the pending window.
+
+## Writing
+
+Visibility is an ordinary write option. Every write path, single track, stream chunk, manifest, batch, threads `WriteOptions.visibility` into the register instruction, so a restricted stream is restricted in every chunk. A plaintext restricted track is a legitimate shape: nodes gate the bytes, the writer trusts the nodes. Confidentiality from nodes is the encryption format in the privacy repo, which seals first and then writes through this same path.
